------------------------------------------------------------------------

This appendix reports the full model output supporting the meso-level analysis in Section 4.3 of the main text. All models use individual and wave fixed effects with standard errors clustered at the respondent level. The outcome throughout is `immigSelf`. Section B.1 presents the main Britishness models for all three constituency-level economic indicators; Section B.2 reports the categorical Britishness robustness check; Section B.3 presents the parallel Englishness models; and Section B.4 reports the categorical Englishness robustness check. A description of the meso-level variables appears in Section B.5.

The meso-level indicators are derived from official ONS sources, the Annual Population Survey (APS) for unemployment rates, administrative claimant-rate records capturing the proportion of working-age residents claiming unemployment-related benefits and the Annual Survey of Hours and Earnings (ASHE) for local earnings, and are merged to the BES panel by local-authority code and fieldwork year. Individual fixed effects control for all time-invariant respondent characteristics, including stable ideological predispositions and baseline nationalism; wave fixed effects absorb common national shocks and period-specific influences such as Brexit milestones, macroeconomic cycles, and the COVID-19 pandemic. Standard errors are clustered at the individual level to account for serial correlation across repeated observations of the same respondents.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

# B.1 Main Models: Britishness (Continuous) {#sec-b1-brit}

## B.1.1 Local Unemployment Rate {#sec-b1-unemp}

Across the baseline specifications, the local unemployment rate exhibits no statistically significant main effect on immigration attitudes once individual and wave fixed effects are included. Changes in local-authority unemployment are therefore not associated with within-person changes in immigration attitudes on average. Unlike the constituency-level analysis, introducing the interaction between unemployment and Britishness provides no evidence of conditional effects. Neither the unemployment coefficient nor the interaction term reaches conventional levels of statistical significance, and the interaction is estimated close to zero across specifications with and without age controls. By contrast, Britishness remains strongly and consistently associated with more restrictive immigration attitudes. Overall, the local-authority results provide no support for the argument that local unemployment conditions condition the relationship between national identification and immigration attitudes, suggesting that the moderation effects observed at the constituency level do not generalise to this broader geographical scale.

```{r b1-unemp-main}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Unemp only"      = m_u0.1,
    "(2) + Britishness"   = m_u1,
    "(3) Unemp + Age"     = m_u0.2,
    "(4) + Britishness"   = m_u2
  ),
  coef_rename = c(
    brit            = "Britishness",
    unemployment_rate = "Unemp. rate (local)",
    age             = "Age"
  ),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B1.1. Local Unemployment Rate and Immigration Attitudes: Main Effects (Britishness)",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigSelf.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

```{r b1-unemp-int}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Interaction"       = m_u3,
    "(2) Interaction + Age" = m_u4
  ),
  coef_rename = c(
    brit                        = "Britishness",
    unemployment_rate             = "Unemp. rate (local)",
    "brit:unemployment_rate"      = "Britishness × Unemp. rate",
    age                         = "Age"
  ),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B1.2. Local Unemployment Rate × Britishness: Interaction Models",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigSelf.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

## B.1.2 Claimant Count {#sec-b1-claimant}

Results for the claimant rate closely parallel those for the local unemployment rate. In models including only main effects, the claimant rate exhibits no statistically significant association with immigration attitudes once individual and wave fixed effects are accounted for. Likewise, introducing the interaction with Britishness provides no evidence of conditional effects. Neither the claimant-rate coefficient nor the interaction term reaches conventional levels of statistical significance, and both remain substantively small across specifications with and without age controls. Britishness, by contrast, remains strongly and consistently associated with more restrictive immigration attitudes. Taken together, these findings indicate that variation in local-authority claimant rates is not associated with within-person changes in immigration attitudes and does not condition the relationship between national identification and immigration preferences. As with the unemployment models, the moderation effects observed in the constituency-level analysis do not replicate when local economic conditions are measured at the local-authority level.

```{r b1-claimant-main}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Claimant only"   = m_c0.1,
    "(2) + Britishness"   = m_c1,
    "(3) Claimant + Age"  = m_c0.2,
    "(4) + Britishness"   = m_c2
  ),
  coef_rename = c(
    brit          = "Britishness",
    claimant_rate = "Claimant count (local)",
    age           = "Age"
  ),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B1.3. Claimant Count and Immigration Attitudes: Main Effects (Britishness)",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigSelf.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

```{r b1-claimant-int}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Interaction"       = m_c3,
    "(2) Interaction + Age" = m_c4
  ),
  coef_rename = c(
    brit                   = "Britishness",
    claimant_rate          = "Claimant count (local)",
    "brit:claimant_rate"   = "Britishness × Claimant count",
    age                    = "Age"
  ),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B1.4. Claimant Count × Britishness: Interaction Models",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigSelf.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

## B.1.3 Local Income (ASHE) {#sec-b1-income}

In contrast to the unemployment and claimant-rate indicators, local earnings display evidence of a conditional relationship with immigration attitudes at the local-authority level. In the baseline specifications, local earnings do not reach conventional levels of statistical significance once individual and wave fixed effects are included, although the estimated coefficients are consistently negative and relatively close to conventional significance thresholds. However, introducing the interaction with Britishness reveals a statistically significant moderation pattern. Higher local earnings are associated with more restrictive immigration attitudes among respondents with lower levels of Britishness, while the negative interaction term indicates that this association weakens as national identification increases. This pattern is highly consistent across specifications with and without age controls, and the interaction coefficients are estimated with considerable precision. Substantively, these results suggest that local earnings levels may shape immigration attitudes in a conditional manner, with the association concentrated among individuals with weaker national identification. Unlike the unemployment and claimant-rate models, the local-authority income measure therefore provides evidence that contextual economic conditions interact with national identity in explaining within-person changes in immigration attitudes, though the positive direction of the earnings effect among low-identity respondents warrants further interpretive attention.

```{r b1-income-main}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Income only"    = m_i0.1,
    "(2) + Britishness"  = m_i1,
    "(3) Income + Age"   = m_i0.2,
    "(4) + Britishness"  = m_i2
  ),
  coef_rename = c(
    brit         = "Britishness",
    earnings_median  = "Local income (ASHE)",
    age          = "Age"
  ),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B1.5. Local Income (ASHE) and Immigration Attitudes: Main Effects (Britishness)",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigSelf.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

```{r b1-income-int}
modelsummary(
