------------------------------------------------------------------------

# B.2 Robustness: Britishness (Categorical) {#sec-b2-brit-cat}

Re-estimating the meso-level models using Britishness as a categorical variable with the "very strongly British" category (7) as the reference broadly confirms the conclusions from the continuous specifications. Britishness category coefficients are positive, precisely estimated, and monotonically ordered across all meso indicators, indicating that respondents with weaker national identification are consistently more permissive toward immigration relative to the most strongly British group.

For local unemployment and claimant count, the categorical interaction models yield no coherent pattern of conditional effects. Interaction terms are generally small and non-significant, with two isolated exceptions: claimant_rate × brit = 1 is negative and marginally significant (β ≈ −0.031, p ≈ 0.017), and unemployment_rate × brit = 6 approaches significance at the 10% level (p ≈ 0.084). These do not constitute a systematic moderation pattern and should be interpreted with caution.

For local income, the categorical interaction models reveal a structured and statistically robust pattern. The interaction terms are positive, significant, and systematically declining in magnitude as Britishness increases toward the reference category, from brit = 1 (β ≈ 0.000894, p \< 0.001) through brit = 6 (β ≈ 0.000228, p ≈ 0.003). This gradient is consistent with the continuous specification and reinforces the interpretation that higher local earnings are associated with more restrictive attitudes primarily among those with weaker national identification, with the effect attenuating as Britishness strengthens.

