# B.4 Robustness: Englishness (Categorical) {#sec-b4-engl-cat}

Re-estimating the meso-level Englishness models with Englishness treated as a categorical variable (reference: 7, "very strongly English") broadly confirms the conclusions from the continuous specifications. Englishness category coefficients are positive, precisely estimated, and monotonically ordered across all meso indicators, indicating that respondents with weaker English identification are consistently more permissive toward immigration relative to the most strongly English group.

For local unemployment, the categorical interaction models yield no significant conditional effects at any identity category, consistent with the null result in the continuous specification. For claimant count, conditional effects are present and significant at weaker identity positions, particularly engl = 1 (β ≈ 0.028, p \< 0.01) and engl = 2 (β ≈ 0.021, p \< 0.05), and attenuate toward zero at higher Englishness levels, consistent with the continuous interaction result. For local income, the categorical interaction models show a similar pattern of attenuation across identity categories, mirroring the modest but significant Englishness income effect observed in the continuous specifications.

