# B.5 Description of Meso-Level Variables {#sec-b5-vars}

The meso-level indicators used in this appendix capture objective local economic conditions at the level of UK local authorities. All variables are derived from official ONS sources, merged to the BES panel using local authority code × fieldwork year, and reshaped to match the wave-based structure of the survey.

**Local unemployment rate (APS).** This variable measures the unemployment rate in each Local Authority, constructed from the Annual Population Survey (APS) Local Labour Market Indicators. The APS unemployment rate captures the proportion of economically active individuals who are unemployed based on rolling survey estimates. For each BES wave, APS data are matched to the relevant fieldwork year to produce a Local Authority-level measure of labour-market conditions that reflects the economic context in which respondents reside rather than their individual employment status.

**Claimant count.** The claimant rate variable measures the proportion of residents claiming unemployment-related benefits within each Local Authority, based on ONS Claimant Count statistics. These data capture individuals receiving Jobseeker’s Allowance or the unemployment-related component of Universal Credit and are available at monthly frequency; monthly observations are aggregated to yearly Local Authority-level averages. Compared with the unemployment rate, the claimant rate reflects a more administrative and welfare-based dimension of economic hardship, capturing local benefit dependency and visible economic distress.

**Local income (ASHE).** Local earnings are measured using median weekly gross pay from the Annual Survey of Hours and Earnings (ASHE), based on place of residence and covering all employees, working patterns, and sexes. The variable captures the typical earnings level of residents within each Local Authority and is merged to the BES panel by Local Authority and fieldwork year. Unlike the unemployment and claimant-rate indicators, this measure reflects broader local economic prosperity rather than labour-market risk or welfare dependency, and tends to vary more gradually over time.

